Boot Process
      Definition: Sequence of steps that occurs from the moment a computer is powered on until the operating system is fully loaded and ready for use.
      Steps      
                  1) Power On
                  2) BIOS (Basic Input/Output System)
                  3) Master Boot Record (MBR) or EFI Parition
                  4) Boot Loader (e.g. GRUB)
                  5) Kernel
                  6) Initial RAM dis - initramfs
                  7) /sbin/init (parent process)
                  8) Command shell using getty
                  9) Graphical User Interface (X Window or Wayland)
      BIOS
                  Definition: 
                              A small program stored on a read-only memory (ROM) chip on a computer’s motherboard. It is the first software that 
                              runs when you power on your computer, even before the operating system is involved.
                  Actions: 
                              The BIOS performs a series of checks and initializations known as the Power-On Self-Test (POST).
                              During POST, the BIOS initializes critical hardware components such as the screen, keyboard, and memory, ensuring that everything is functioning correctly before the operating system loads.
                              The BIOS also finds and loads the bootloader, which then starts the operating system.
                              Once the BIOS finishes its checks, it hands control over to the next stage of the boot process, which is managed by the operating system.
                  BLUF:
                              The BIOS acts as a bridge between the computer’s hardware and its operating system, ensuring that everything is ready for the OS to take over.
      MBR, EFI Partition & Boot Loader
                  Definition: 
                              Boot loader is small program stored on system's storage on hard disk or solid state drive typically.
                  Location:
                              On traditional BIOS/MBR systems, the boot loader resides in the boot sector. 
                              On more modern systems that use the (Unified) Extensible Firmware Interface (EFI or UEFI), it is stored in the EFI partition.
                  Actions:
                              Afterward, the system retrieves information such as the current date, time, and hardware configuration from the CMOS. 
                              This small battery-powered memory chip retains data even when the system is powered off.
                  Common Boot Loader:
                              GRUB (GRand Unified Boot Loader) – the most common boot loader for Linux distributions
                  Options:
                              Most Linux boot loaders provide a menu or user interface that allows you to choose between multiple operating systems or different Linux kernel versions. 
                              When you select a Linux option, the boot loader’s main job is to load the kernel image and the initial RAM disk (initrd or initramfs) into memory. 
                              These components contain essential drivers and files that keep the system booting until the whole operating system is loaded.
                  BIOS Boot Process:
                              BIOS -> MBR -> Boot Loader -> Kernel -> OS
                  UEFI Boot Process:
                              UEFI -> EFI Boot Loader -> Kernel - OS
                  Commands
                              Check Vendor Version = sudo dmidecode -t bios
                              Check if using BIOS or UEFI = [ -d /sys/firmware/efi ] && echo "UEFI" || echo "Legacy BIOS"
      Manual
