Understanding Linux Filesystem
      Paritions and Filesystems
                  physical storage =  the hardware, physical "container", a fixed area to be managed as a single unit
                  logical organization = the software, the logical "method" of storage 
                  partition  = represents a dedicated subsection of hard drive, the logical "method" of storage, partitions are identified as device files, such as /dev/sda1 for the first partition on your primary drive.
                  filesystem = the logical structure that stores and accesses files within that space.
                  "formatting" a partition = installing a specific filesystem, such as ext4 or XFS, to organize that space.
                  Generally, a filesystem resides within a single partition; however, in some circumstances, a filesystem can span more than one partition using symbolic links
                  mounting = "attach" the filesystem of a partition to a specific directory (mount point). For example, you might mount the partition /dev/sdb1 to the directory /home. From that moment on, any file you save in your home folder is physically written to that specific partition. 
