      Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS)
                  Definition: a specification that establishes the names, locations, and intended purposes of directories across Linux
                  Removable Media: removable media such as USB drives and optical discs are also accessed through the filesystem tree rather than through separate drive letters. 
                  / = highest level filesystem
                  /root = superuser's administrator's account
                  /home = contains user accounts, regular users' personal/home directories are usually stored.
                  /etc = system-wide configuration files. If an application needs a settings file that affects all users, it lives here. 
                  /usr/bin (/bin) = essential user-facing programs and commands (e.g., ls, cp, bash). On most modern distributions, /bin is a symbolic link to /usr/bin 
                  /usr/sbin (/sbin) = system administration programs intended for use by the root user. 
                  /usr/lib (/lib) = shared libraries required by programs in /bin and /sbin
                  /var = variable data that changes during normal operation, such as log files (/var/log), print queues, and mail spools.
                  /tmp = temporary files created by applications. These are not guaranteed to survive a reboot.
                  /dev = device files. Linux represents hardware (hard drives, terminals, USB devices) as files in this directory
                  /proc = a virtual filesystem that exposes real-time information about running processes and the kernel. Nothing here is stored on disk; it is generated on demand. 
                  /sys = a virtual filesystem providing a structured interface to kernel and hardware information.
                  /boot = files required to start the system, including the Linux kernel itself. 
                  /run = runtime data for processes started since the last boot, such as process ID files and lock files
      Case Sensitivity
                  Linux is case sensitive so uppercase and lowercase letters are seen as distinct.
                  Convention: use lowercase letters when creating directories
      The /usr Directory
                  Definition:  originally stood for "Unix System Resources", and today it is best understood as the home of the operating system's general-purpose software layer not required for booting the system.
                               directories directly under / contain only what is strictly necessary to boot the system and perform basic recovery tasks: the minimum needed before anything else is loaded.               
                  /usr/bin   = the vast majority of user-facing commands and programs.
                  /usr/sbin  = system administration tools not required at boot.
                  /usr/lib   = shared libraries for programs in /usr/bin and /usr/sbin.
                  /usr/share = architecture-independent data such as documentation and icons.
                  /usr/local = software installed manually by the system administrator, outside the distribution's package manager.
                  Key Takeaway: when you are looking for a program or tool on a Linux system, /usr/bin is the first place to look. 
                                if you have installed software manually or compiled something from source, check /usr/local/bin.
